   "source": [
    "1. What is Machine Learning?\n",
    "Machine Learning (ML) is a branch of Artificial Intelligence that enables computers to learn from data and make predictions or decisions without being explicitly programmed for every situation.\n",
    "Example: A computer learns from past house prices and predicts the price of a new house."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "fc031236-23f1-453b-ad40-3849bb345d7c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "2. What is a Machine Learning Algorithm?\n",
    "A Machine Learning algorithm is a set of mathematical rules or instructions used by a computer to learn patterns from data and build a model.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example: Linear Regression is a Machine Learning algorithm used to predict numerical values such as house prices, sales, and marks."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "78c6bbb0-365a-413b-8576-91fabe34dc10",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "3. How does Machine Learning work?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "dd5439e8-fb02-421b-94d8-177e68ada82a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "4. Types of Machine Learning Algorithms\n",
    "Type\tExplanation\tExample\n",
    "Supervised Learning\tLearns from labelled data\tPredicting student marks\n",
    "Unsupervised Learning\tFinds patterns in unlabelled data\tGrouping customers\n",
    "Semi-supervised Learning\tLearns from a small amount of labelled data and a large amount of unlabelled data\tImage classification\n",
    "Reinforcement Learning\tLearns through rewards and penalties\tTraining a robot to walk"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "89620fdd-346d-406e-88d2-3360afc0e797",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "1. Linear Regression \n",
    "Linear Regression is a Supervised Machine Learning algorithm because it learns from labelled data containing input values (features) and known output values (target).\n",
    "\n",
    "It learns the relationship between variables and uses that relationship to predict numerical values.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example: Learning the relationship between study hours and marks to predict a student's marks based on study hours."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "7b4cafc2-28dd-4b6c-8624-20c8b53d4501",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "2.\"Can we predict a student's marks based on the number of hours they study?\"\n",
    "Write the following example on the board:\n",
    "Study Hours(X)     \t Marks(Y)\n",
    "1\t                     35\n",
    "2\t                     45\n",
    "3\t                     55\n",
    "4\t                     65\n",
    "5\t                     75\n",
    "\n",
    "•\tAs study hours increase, marks also increase.\n",
    "•\tWe can observe a relationship between study hours and marks.\n",
    "•\tCan we predict the marks of a student who studies for 6 hours?\n",
    "•\tMachine Learning uses algorithms like Linear Regression to make such predictions.\n",
    "---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "3. What is Linear Regression? \n",
    "Linear Regression is a supervised Machine Learning algorithm used to predict a continuous numerical value by identifying the linear relationship between independent and dependent variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "•\tSupervised Learning: The algorithm learns from data containing known input and output values.\n",
    "•\tLinear: The relationship is represented by a straight line.\n",
    "•\tRegression: It predicts numerical values such as marks, salary, sales, or temperature.\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "•\tPredicting marks based on study hours.\n",
    "•\tPredicting house prices based on area.\n",
    "•\tPredicting sales based on advertising expenditure.\n",
    "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "4. Independent and Dependent Variables \n",
    "Explain the two important variables:\n",
    "Variable\t                         Meaning\t             Example\n",
    "Independent variable (X)\tInput used for prediction\t   Study hours\n",
    "Dependent variable (Y)\t    Output to be predicted\t       Marks\n",
    "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "5. Linear Regression Equation \n",
    "The equation of a straight line is:\n",
    "y=mx+C\n",
    "Symbol\tMeaning\n",
    "y\tPredicted output\n",
    "x\tInput variable\n",
    "c\tIntercept\n",
    "m\tSlope (coefficient)\n",
    "\n",
    "Slope m: Indicates how much the predicted output changes when the input increases by one unit.\n",
    "Intercept c: The predicted value of Y when X is zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "•\tIntercept = 25\n",
    "•\tSlope = 10\n",
    "If a student studies for 5 hours: predict y?\n",
    "---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "6. Best-Fit Line and Residuals \n",
    "Explain that many straight lines can be drawn through a scatter plot. The algorithm needs to find the line that best represents the data.\n",
    "What is a Best-Fit Line?\n",
    "The best-fit line is the line that minimizes the overall difference between actual values and predicted values.\n",
    "What is a Residual?\n",
    "Residual = Actual Value-Predicted Value\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "Actual Marks\tPredicted Marks\tResidual\n",
    "35\t                35          \t0\n",
    "45\t                45\t            0\n",
    "55\t                55\t            0\n",
    "65\t                65          \t0\n",
    "75\t                75\t            0\n",
    "-------------------------------------------------\n",
    "30\t                35\t           -5\n",
    "40\t                45\t           -5\n",
    "50\t                50\t            0\n",
    "60\t                55\t            5\n",
    "70\t                55\t            5\n",
    "\n",
    "When is the Best-Fit Line Calculated?\n",
    "The best-fit line is calculated during the training process of a Linear Regression model.\n",
    "The model calculates the best-fit line by learning the appropriate values of:\n",
    "- Slope (m): How much Y changes when X increases by one unit.\n",
    "- Intercept (c): The predicted value of Y when X is zero.\n",
    "-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "Linear Regression uses a method called Ordinary Least Squares (OLS).\n",
    "\n",
    "The process is:\n",
    "1. The model considers possible straight lines through the data.\n",
    "2. For each line, it calculates predicted values.\n",
    "3. It calculates the difference between actual and predicted values. This difference is called the residual.\n",
    "4. It squares the residuals and adds them together.\n",
    "5. It finds the line that produces the minimum sum of squared residuals.\n",
    "-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "How is the Best-Fit Line Related to Evaluation Metrics?\n",
    "During training\t                                    After training\n",
    "The model calculates the best-fit line.\t      The model makes predictions.\n",
    "It minimizes the sum of squared residuals.    We calculate MAE, MSE, or RMSE.\n",
    "The model learns the slope and intercept.\t  We measure prediction accuracy.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "91155e13-a708-4013-ad3c-82913e38719b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "How do we know whether the model fits well?\n",
    "Evaluation metris is used\n",
    "MAE?\n",
    "MAE (Mean Absolute Error) measures the average difference between actual values and predicted values in a Machine Learning model.\n",
    "It tells us, on average, how far the model's predictions are from the actual values.\n",
    "Actual Marks\tPredicted Marks\tError (Actual − Predicted)\tAbsolute Error\n",
    "50\t                 55                  \t-5\t                5\n",
    "60\t                 58\t                     2\t                2\n",
    "70\t                 75\t                    -5\t                5\n",
    "80\t                 78\t                     2\t                2\n",
    "Total\t\t\t                                               14\n",
    "- Negative error means the predicted value is greater than the actual value.\n",
    "- Positive error means the predicted value is less than the actual value.\n",
    "- MAE converts all errors into positive values and calculates their average.\n",
    "Interpretation: The model's predictions are off by an average of 3.5 marks."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "1ecd5a5e-9270-41b2-aae8-8e4c42924043",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Reason for squaring errors\n",
    "1. To avoid positive and negative errors cancelling each other\n",
    "2. To penalize large errors more strongly\n",
    "- MSE uses squared errors.\n",
    "- RMSE takes the square root of MSE.\n",
    "- MAE uses absolute errors instead of squared errors, so it does not penalize large errors as strongly."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "8970d753-e5f2-4797-a296-ff15b70fe149",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "MSE (Mean Squared Error) measures the average of the squared differences between actual and predicted values.\n",
    "Actual\tPredicted\tError (Actual − Predicted)\tSquared Error\n",
    "50\t       55\t           -5\t                   25\n",
    "60\t       58\t            2\t                   4\n",
    "70         75\t           -5\t                   25\n",
    "80\t       78\t            2\t                    4\n",
    "Total\t\t\t                                    58(marks square)\n",
    "Why square the errors? Squaring makes negative errors positive and gives larger errors more importance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "f811ddc9-86b0-4d1d-816e-6f72340c6c4e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error) measures how far a model’s predictions are from the actual values, on average. It is the square root of MSE.\n",
    "The main benefit of taking the square root of MSE is that RMSE becomes easier to understand.\n",
    "Example\n",
    "- MSE = 14.5 marks² — the unit is squared, so it is difficult to interpret.\n",
    "- RMSE = √14.5 ≈ 3.81 marks — the unit is the same as the original target (marks).\n",
    "In simple words: RMSE tells us the typical size of prediction errors in the original unit, making it easier to understand how far predictions are from actual values."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "e4ed10a8-5459-494f-903b-41883577f71a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "R² Score\n",
    "R² Score (R-squared score) is an evaluation metric used to measure how well a linear regression model explains the variation \n",
    "in the target variable.\n",
    "1. SSres – Sum of Squared Residuals\n",
    "SSres measures how far the predicted values are from the actual values.\n",
    "\n",
    "Residual = Actual value − Predicted value\n",
    "\n",
    "We square each residual to avoid negative errors cancelling positive errors.\n",
    "Student\tActual (y)\tPredicted (ŷ)\tResidual (y − ŷ)\tSquared Residual\n",
    "A\t     50\t          52\t             −2\t                 4\n",
    "B\t     60\t          58\t              2\t                 4\n",
    "C\t     70\t          72\t             −2\t                 4\n",
    "D\t     80\t          78\t              2\t                 4\n",
    "E\t     90\t          88\t              2\t                 4\n",
    "Total\t\t\t\t20\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore,\n",
    "SS(res) = 4+4+4+4+4 = 20\n",
    "\n",
    "2. SStot – Total Sum of Squares\n",
    "SStot measures how much the actual values vary from their mean (average).\n",
    "\n",
    "First, calculate the mean of actual marks:\n",
    "SS(tot)= (50+60+70+80+90)/5=70\n",
    "Student\tActual (y)\tMean (ȳ)\tDifference (y − ȳ)\tSquared Difference\n",
    "A\t      50\t     70\t            −20\t                  400\n",
    "B\t      60\t     70\t            −10\t                  100\n",
    "C\t      70\t     70\t              0\t                   0\n",
    "D\t      80\t     70\t             10\t                  100\n",
    "E\t      90\t     70\t             20\t                  400\n",
    "Total\t\t\t\t                                     1000\n",
    "Therefore,\n",
    "SS_(tot)=400+100+0+100+400=1000\n",
    "\n",
    "                                               R2(score)=1-SS(res)/SS(tot)\n",
    "                                                 =1-20/1000 = 1-0.02 =0.98\n",
    "\n",
    "SSres\tHow much error our model makes\n",
    "SStot\tHow much the actual data varies from its mean\n",
    "R²\tHow much better our model explains the data compared with simply predicting the mean.\n",
    "-------------------------\n",
    "R² Score Interpretation\n",
    "-------------------------\n",
    "R² Score\tMeaning\n",
    "1.0 (100%)\tPerfect prediction\n",
    "0.9 (90%)\tModel explains 90% of the variation\n",
    "0.7 (70%)\tModel explains 70% of the variation\n",
    "0.0\tModel is no better than predicting the mean\n",
    "Negative value\tModel performs worse than predicting the mean.\n",
    "From the above example,\n",
    "R² = 98%: The model explains 98% of the variation in the actual data.\n",
    "MAE, MSE, RMSE: Tell us how far the predicted values are from the actual values."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "1095f3db-6fb7-4883-b3ff-1c9cea85915f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is Adjusted R² Score?\n",
    "Adjusted R² score is an evaluation metric used in multiple linear regression that measures how well the model explains the variation in the target variable, while also considering the number of independent variables (features) used in the model.\n",
    "Why is it needed?\n",
    "In multiple linear regression, when we add more independent variables to a model, the normal R² score usually increases or remains the same—even if the added variable is not useful.\n",
    "\n",
    "    formula adjusted r2score = 1-(1-R2score)*[(n-1)/(n-p-1)]\n",
    "R2score --\tR² score of the model\n",
    "n\t --     Number of observations (rows)\n",
    "p\t-----   Number of independent variables (features)\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose we predict house prices using the following features:\n",
    "Model\t   Features Used\t                R² Score   \tAdjusted R²\n",
    "Model 1\t    Area\t                          0.80\t     0.79\n",
    "Model 2     Area, Bedrooms\t                  0.85\t     0.83\n",
    "Model 3\t    Area,Bedrooms,Random ID           0.86\t     0.82\n",
    "Note:R² tells us how well the model fits the data, while Adjusted R² tells us whether adding more features actually improves the model enough to justify their inclusion."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "83bac8ed-7a9b-42d7-a3f4-e8aecdab7470",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) is an evaluation metric used in regression to measure the average \n",
    "prediction error as a percentage of the actual values.\n",
    "It tells us, on average, how far the predicted values are from the actual values in percentage terms.\n",
    "Example\n",
    "Suppose a model predicts students' marks:\n",
    "Student        \tActual Marks\t     Predicted Marks\t       Absolute Percentage Error\n",
    "A\t               50\t                  45\t                       10%\n",
    "B\t               60\t                  66\t                       10%\n",
    "C\t               80\t                  72\t                       10%\n",
    "D\t              100\t                  90\t                       10%\n",
    "                                               Average\t               10%\n",
    "\n",
    "For each student:\n",
    "                              APE=[(Actual Value-Predicted Value)/Actual Value] *100 = [(50-45)/50]*100\n",
    "\n",
    "MAPE = 10% means the predicted marks differ from the actual marks by an average of 10%, in absolute percentage terms.\n",
    "Important limitation\n",
    "MAPE is not suitable when actual values are zero, because division by zero is undefined. It can also become misleading when actual values are very close to zero.\n",
    "Note: Scikit-learn returns MAPE as a fraction, so multiply by 100 to express it as a percentage.\n",
    "A good regression model generally has low MAE, MSE, RMSE and MAPE, and a high R² score, when evaluated on suitable test data.\n"
   ]
  },
